#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124339 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "dfb93cc8-6bf0-59d4-8fbc-c94f5c0c1a69" :question/math-academy-id "e-5502" :question/problem "Find $A^{100},$ where \n![](images/66/66521b0220a4a850308d42aeccf17ce4769e563eeec6a7e1870adbcb0f46a318.png)" :question/worked-solution "First,  notice that\n\n![](images/9c/9c5de663d2cf109c4faaf839760b0afac6dcbf5ab533570100167727853cb74f.png)\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/3f/3f73ff47f00cf345f0ac0cffd41dc325faf00c9f1d1c0ffe4ad7228e83fef3fa.png)\n\nSince $A=PDP^{-1},$ we can compute $A^k$ by using the formula \n\n$$\nA^{k} = PD^{k}P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{100} & =P{D}^{100}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{100}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{100} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {0}^{100}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124340 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "737021ad-5198-564f-bbe2-cc5a06ee5a82" :question/math-academy-id "e-5503" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $E,$ given that \n![](images/33/3389ba82baf25977d7b8deb9609435dce5a543a863f7d8f98102b45d770b465b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/9d/9dee992fa1cc903eb23299b52080a7ebba31ef550bb22c4938c45939e8541be4.png)\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=6$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n![](images/23/23dd5ab30b31bf85d84f290fdf9c71529cfd753b98688149f103980894ad91c6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124381 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "c1ebc3ac-7a26-565f-9798-9de531dc6e47" :question/math-academy-id "e-7133" :question/problem "Find a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n![](images/f4/f478b4206f3a8bcc5c025fa0c58bb75430bcb1493c1d8470366864f7fb7ceb6e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathcal{B}= \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PDP^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order: \n\n![](images/1c/1cf1ba5d93618341227836a110479ebfc258894f9d4f60ae4df1be682d7c74fc.png)\n\nLet ![](images/f1/f1defa7b451fcd56632030e9c3663ad99646c5eabc98eb4e1c5b687687b9fdc4.png) Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166c0aaf-b985-5233-8dc4-1bc4351cc79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321330} {:answer/id #uuid "44e4a671-6b0b-5a06-9dee-675f029f9d8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321693} {:answer/id #uuid "56cdacb6-cf3c-5add-9a8a-059378ce6389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321832} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8743be-e0bc-5124-b1cf-b7fa9ac4a406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322397} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5112d5-6501-5876-951e-7cf715721940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "166c0aaf-b985-5233-8dc4-1bc4351cc79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321330} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1dac96b-14a8-5dbc-a8c3-514fd151d125" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca2ed65a-f922-59dc-89be-4cf00704985d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37363" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $L$ given that \n\n$$\nL[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $L:$\n\n$$\nL={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $L$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $L$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3900588-aa11-57c6-abd1-75c5a2ab0223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123111} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b25bd8-15e0-5843-b7e7-b658a5543659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}97 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -97\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123112} {:answer/id #uuid "3156cdd3-c948-5416-89d8-33273c679d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123113} {:answer/id #uuid "56a968bd-7dfb-55d3-a4eb-b423eadf056c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123114} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5d510d-721e-52fb-b7c4-9f1837af0ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123115}] :answer-field/id #uuid "94a05194-4704-5922-a7f4-370e8db021bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d5f0e3f-05e4-5b32-8e42-33db9fecfeec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392116} {:answer/id #uuid "afc41b45-077b-59fa-a6a2-e28ef564d7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392117} {:answer/id #uuid "e32ce8d9-f253-5c0c-9791-88ffd609ec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392118} {:answer/id #uuid "d874ecd1-ed8a-514f-b148-263bd1e5349a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}97 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -97\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392119} {:answer/id #uuid "cd20040e-34e4-5440-afc8-b39be1193ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e32ce8d9-f253-5c0c-9791-88ffd609ec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392118} :answer-field/id #uuid "f73eb74f-7d8e-56f2-a6c1-4e42926c09f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7259ad7-f6a1-5f38-87c9-86ba246db7b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-37597" :question/problem "Find ${A}^{97},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{97} & =P{D}^{97}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{97}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{97} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{97}\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f13d971-9265-51dc-ba11-ae2ddbe5134a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322147} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb2f5e6-516f-5722-8eb5-dac98c70a330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-32 & -12 \\\\ 12 & -13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322383} {:answer/id #uuid "c90e3b36-b996-5161-8084-e491b59e729b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}24 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322713} {:answer/id #uuid "ec347ab6-adc8-5ea1-bec1-f80c82d90acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 48 \\\\ 12 & 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323000} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7b780c-5ca5-52ec-97db-7fc97d7bcf98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 72 \\\\ -24 & 52\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef7b780c-5ca5-52ec-97db-7fc97d7bcf98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 72 \\\\ -24 & 52\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323033} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c8c700d-19c2-58d3-8524-ed5379c8ddea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2975c4f9-6dee-5125-bb13-6ec3dea74ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37601" :question/problem "Find ${M}^{16},$ where\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{16} & =P{D}^{16}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{16}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt[8]{2})^{16} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[4]{2})^{16}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{4}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 48 \\\\ 4 & 32\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 72 \\\\ -24 & 52\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce48fd3-6d90-5867-bc8c-48f781a9a134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351673} {:answer/id #uuid "6dea34ce-51cc-54ce-bd9e-2e2c5ec68b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352535} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac80d5-5537-5ad5-953b-dc791c6b3dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352561} {:answer/id #uuid "7239e333-3699-5e0e-a679-3638ec2f4a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 21 & -32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352567} {:answer/id #uuid "7b23a21b-03eb-5d9a-8c5d-1e2dbf0ce327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -19 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ce48fd3-6d90-5867-bc8c-48f781a9a134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351673} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ed20fe-9723-5a36-b174-23a11d61985b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1fcc8b2-84cd-574b-a993-68fbd5d3c967" :question/math-academy-id "q-86680" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -15 \\\\ 12 & -30\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
